<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Lützen (1632)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Lützen · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Lützen was fought in 1632 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> between the Swedish army and imperial forces under Wallenstein. The Swedes held the field, but King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> was killed during the fighting. The battle is a landmark in the military history of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a>, whose reformed army under Gustavus had transformed European warfare.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lutzen-1632.jpg" alt="Battle of Lützen (1632)" width="320" height="217" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Wahlbom · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Death_of_King_Gustav_II_Adolf_of_Sweden_at_the_Battle_of_L%C3%BCtzen_(Carl_Wahlbom)_-_Nationalmuseum_-_18031.tif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He won Ingria from Russia by the Treaty of Stolbovo in 1617, took Riga in 1621 and in 1630 entered the Thirty Years’ War, winning the Battle of Breitenfeld in 1631 before he was killed at <b>Lützen</b> in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At Breitenfeld (1631), Swedish and Saxon forces routed Tilly's veteran Catholic League army, and at <b>Lützen</b> (1632) the Swedes held the field against Wallenstein even though Gustavus himself was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>King Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden, then campaigning in Germany, signed the foundation act of a university in Dorpat (Tartu), months before his death at <b>Lützen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-30">30 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>A Swedish army under King Gustavus Adolphus, supported by troops from Saxony and Hesse-Kassel, narrowly defeated an imperial force under Albrecht von Wallenstein at <b>Lützen</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-16">16 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Gottfried Heinrich of Pappenheim was a leading imperial cavalry commander in the Thirty Years' War, killed at <b>Lützen</b> in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-pappenheim">Lordship of Pappenheim</a></li></ul></section>
